// Card 2 reuses the moving-out-day photo, which does honestly show what that
// card is about. See IMAGE-PROMPTS.md in this folder for the reasoning.
// Section 2 backdrop behind the Cream scrim, shared with the trust bar.
import footerLogoImg from '../../assets/logo/tlb-logo-square.png';
import {
  primaryNav,
  headerNav,
  headerSecondaryCta,
  footerServiceLinks,
  footerContact,
  footerCopyright,
  quoteCta,
} from '../../data/navigation';
import { trustBarCards } from '../../data/trust';
import { howItWorks, managedForYou, theLineBody } from '../../data/brand-lines';
import { localBusiness } from '../../data/business';
import { z } from 'astro/zod';
import { getPage } from '../../lib/cms';
import * as section from '../../lib/cms-schemas';
import { sized, highlightColumn } from '../../lib/cms-schemas';

// Section 0. Page metadata.
//
// The description names the humidity, which is the local hook and the honest
// reason this service exists here rather than a generic one. It claims
// removal of surface mould specifically, not remediation, and promises
// nothing about it staying away.
//
// ⚠️ BOTH REWRITTEN 15 Sep 2026 (copy and search review of this page).
//
// TITLE. This is the approved title from mapping workbook row 6, which was
// never implemented. It carries the mapped primary `mould cleaning services`
// (350/mo, mapping workbook, 10 Aug 2026) unsplit, and "Removal" carries the
// mapped secondary `mould removal services`. 48 characters, no decoration.
//
// 🚩 DESCRIPTION SET FROM THE KEYWORD WORKBOOK 16 Sep 2026, on TLB's
// explicit instruction to ship the workbook copy verbatim. 145 characters,
// matching the workbook's own length column exactly. This reverses the
// 15 Sep ruling recorded here, which replaced the workbook line rather than
// implementing it. That objection was not withdrawn on its merits, so it
// stands on the record and TLB has accepted it:
//
//  1. ⚠️ "Book an inspection today" is flatly contradicted by section 4 of
//     this page, which says "Not an inspection and not a report". A visitor
//     who clicks the SERP snippet to book an inspection lands on copy
//     telling them we do not do one. These two cannot both be right; the
//     body copy was left as-is because the instruction was to change the
//     meta, not the page. Whoever answers the phone should expect callers
//     asking to book the inspection this snippet offers.
//  2. The mapped primary `mould cleaning services` (350/mo, mapping
//     workbook, 10 Aug 2026) is not contiguous in this line; "Mould
//     cleaning and removal" is the closest it gets. The title still carries
//     the primary verbatim.
//  3. The standing geo phrase is gone, replaced by "humid coastal homes".
//     That is a climate descriptor, not a service area, so this page's meta
//     now names no footprint. It also retires the Tweed-vs-Queensland trade
//     this comment used to flag: there is no geo phrase left to extend.
//  4. "Done safely and properly" sits next to section 4's deliberate
//     refusal to publish a method. It claims a standard the page will not
//     detail, which is defensible but is the sort of line to have an answer
//     ready for.

// ⚠️ PAGE-LOCAL CTA LABEL, 15 Sep 2026. The shared `quoteCta` still reads
// "Get an instant quote" everywhere, and on THIS page that promised the one
// thing the copy then withdraws: section 12 says outright that condition
// sets the price and a job like this cannot be priced sight unseen, and the
// whole page is built on sending a photo first. A button offering an instant
// number contradicted the sentence above it.
//
// Overridden here rather than in navigation.ts because that export serves
// every page, including the genuinely instant-priceable ones. The site-wide
// split is still the Phase 2.3 / step 7 job in WEBSITE-UPDATE-PLAN-BFD-V2.md.
// `href` is unchanged, so it routes exactly where it did.
//
// Four instances on this page take it: the hero, pathway card 1, the booking
// block and the closing CTA. Header and footer chrome keep the shared label
// deliberately, same call deep-cleaning.astro made: that is site navigation,
// not this page's argument, and the conflict between the two header CTAs is
// a site-wide decision above this page (BFD §9.5 and §13.2 currently
// contradict each other on it).
const pageQuoteCta = { ...quoteCta, label: 'Get a fast quote' };

// ⚠️ HERO TAKES ITS OWN LABEL, 16 Sep 2026, on a client instruction. The hero
// button now reads "Book an inspection today" rather than "Get a fast quote".
// This is the one place on the page where the label matters most, and it is
// the label that matches what the page actually asks a reader to do: section
// 12 says condition sets the price and a job like this cannot be priced
// sight unseen, so the first ask is a look at the job, not a number. The meta
// description already closed on this exact sentence, so the button and the
// search snippet now say the same thing.
//
// The other three instances (pathway card 1, the booking block and the
// closing CTA) deliberately KEEP "Get a fast quote": by the time a reader is
// that far down they have read the inspection argument, and repeating the
// same booking verb four times reads as a single nagging button rather than
// as a page. `href` is unchanged, so it routes exactly where the others do.
const heroQuoteCta = { ...quoteCta, label: 'Book an inspection today' };

// Section 1. Hero. The figure is this folder's own cut-out rather than the
// homepage cleaner, which would make this page look like the homepage rather
// than like a mould page. It shows work in progress in a clean, ordinary
// bathroom, not a ruined one: the page's argument is that mould is a
// moisture problem in a normal coastal house, and a grim hero would
// contradict the copy and shame the reader section 6 goes out of its way not
// to shame.
//
// The headline is the page's whole honest position in six words, and the
// lead does the thing a competitor's hero will not: it says out loud that
// cleaning alone does not keep it away. That is a strange thing to lead
// with commercially and it is the right thing to lead with here, because the
// alternative is a promise the business cannot keep.
//
// ⚠️ H1 GAINED ONE WORD, 15 Sep 2026. "Keeping it off takes more than
// cleaning" carried no contiguous service phrase at all; "more than mould
// cleaning" lands `mould cleaning` (700/mo, mapping workbook, 10 Aug 2026)
// without touching the rhythm, the meaning or the voice beat. The review
// that prompted this proposed replacing the H1 outright and demoting this
// line to a subheading, which was overridden: the standing rule is the
// smallest edit that lands the service term, and one word does it.
//
// That note used to end "the H1 carries `mould cleaning` but not the full
// primary `mould cleaning services`, and that phrase belongs in the title,
// the lead and the definition paragraph". Overruled 16 Sep 2026 — see below.
//
// ⚠️ LEAD REWRITTEN the same day. It opened on "Surface mould cleaned from",
// which scored zero on the primary. It now opens on the service as people
// search for it and keeps everything that came after it, The Line included.
//
// ⚠️ H1 AND LEAD BOTH REWRITTEN AGAIN, 16 Sep 2026, on an explicit client
// instruction to carry more of the focus keyword in the heading and the
// subheading. This supersedes the "smallest edit that lands the service
// term" ruling recorded above — that rule is why the 15 Sep pass changed one
// word, and the client has since asked for the opposite.
//
// The H1 was two lines, "We can get the mould off." / "Keeping it off takes
// more than mould cleaning." It now names both mapped terms, `mould cleaning`
// and `mould removal`, and lands the same promise in "that gets it off".
//
// ⚠️ CUT TO ONE LINE, client's wording. It went two lines → one sentence
// joined on "but" → a shorter paraphrase → the client's own copy verbatim. No
// full stop: their line, their punctuation.
//
// ⚠️ ONE WORD ADDED BACK, 16 Sep 2026, on the client's instruction: the
// primary is `mould cleaning services` (350/mo, mapping workbook, 10 Aug
// 2026) and the H1 was landing only the `mould cleaning` fragment of it.
// "services" makes the H1 carry the full phrase, and `mould removal` is
// still there in the same breath. This is the ONLY edit to their line.
//
// This drops the second beat entirely — the honest limit that the old line 2
// carried ("keeping it off takes more than mould cleaning"). That position is
// NOT lost from the page: the hero lead says "on the surfaces you can see",
// section 5 refers remediation out, section 8 `#moisture` is the whole
// argument, and section 9's table draws the boundary again. The H1 no longer
// states it, which is a deliberate client call, not an oversight. Do not
// "fix" it back without asking.
//
// The lead's second sentence was "We remove what is there, and we tell you
// honestly what is feeding it." It now opens on `mould removal` and says
// "on the surfaces you can see", which lands the second term in the
// subheading AND pulls the surface-work-not-remediation boundary forward out
// of the definition paragraph. The honesty beat is unchanged in substance.
// The Line itself is untouched: the standing pricing rule sets "Easy to
// book, fast quoting, cost effective" for every non-regular service page and
// this page already carried it, so the review's proposal to change "fast
// quoting" to "fast quote" here does not apply. Only the button changes.
//
// In `hero`, on "Cleaner reaching up with a cloth to wipe down a kitchen window frame":
// A real TLB photo since 6 Oct 2026, replacing the transparent cut-out
// (which ran with `contain-bottom`). Default `cover` fit now. Same photo
// as the end of lease page's "I manage the property" card, but a square
// crop of the logo-free original rather than that card's 4:3: measured at
// 1440px the desktop panel is roughly square (~650x615), and the 4:3 file
// lost a fifth of its width there. Square, it shows the full frame, cloth
// to bench. `ratio` matches it on mobile.

// Section 2. Definition paragraph. Answer-target for `mould cleaning` and
// `mould removal`, and the GEO passage most likely to be lifted whole by an
// AI overview. BFD 3.6.2 asks for a definition-style opening sentence and a
// semantic triple, which is what the two sentences do.
//
// The definition is deliberately mechanical, not medical: what mould is and
// what it needs to grow. No health framing anywhere in it. See the file
// header, constraint 1.
//
// ⚠️ THIRD SENTENCE REWRITTEN 15 Sep 2026, and nothing else in this block.
// It is the page's own definition of the service and the sentence most
// likely to be lifted whole as a direct answer, so it should name the
// service the way people search for it and name the limit in the same
// breath. It now carries the mapped primary `mould cleaning services`
// (350/mo, mapping workbook, 10 Aug 2026) and ends "on surfaces rather than
// inside walls", which keeps the scope boundary inside the sentence an AI
// overview is most likely to quote on its own. BFD §14.
//
// The second paragraph is untouched on purpose: it handles the mapped
// secondary `mould removal services` honestly and it is the clearest passage
// on the page.

// Section 3. Pathway cards. Three genuinely different visitors, and the
// third one is the reason this page is written the way it is: someone whose
// mould keeps coming back has usually already paid someone to clean it.
//
// Card 3 lands on section 8, the moisture section, via #moisture set on a
// wrapper in the template below. Same anchor pattern as the deep-cleaning
// and end-of-lease pages use for #compare.
//
// Section heading/accent and the three CTA labels are DRAFTED, not briefed.
//
// In `pathwayCards`, on the button of "It's in the bathroom and it looks awful":
// Page-local "Get a fast quote", 15 Sep 2026 — see `pageQuoteCta`.
// Cards 2 and 3 are unchanged: they route to another page and to an
// anchor, not to the quote flow.

// Section 4. Where we find it and what we do about it.
//
// [CONFIRM THE WHOLE LIST WITH TEAGAN.] Drafted from category convention,
// not from TLB's scope of works. The surfaces are safe enough to describe,
// but two lines below make operational commitments that need checking:
// whether the team treats silicone and grout (as opposed to declaring them
// beyond cleaning), and whether soft furnishings are touched at all.
//
// SHAPE. ContentGrid `text` cells at 2 columns, matching the inclusions
// grids on the deep-cleaning and end-of-lease pages so all three read as one
// company's standard. Six cells, three clean rows, no span needed.
//
// The copy deliberately describes WHERE and WHAT, never HOW. No product
// names, no methods, no dilutions. See the file header, safety copy.

// Section 5. What sits outside the clean, and when we hand it on.
//
// [CONFIRM]. This Callout draws the commercial boundary of the whole page,
// and it is the block most likely to be softened by someone who wants the
// page to sell harder. Resist that. A cleaning company that quietly takes on
// a leak-driven or in-cavity mould job is selling a clean it knows will fail
// within a month, which is both a bad outcome and, under BFD 5.3, a claim
// problem.
//
// The second paragraph names the trades to call. That is deliberate: telling
// someone the job is not yours without telling them whose it is reads as a
// brush-off rather than as advice.

// Section 6. Why it happens here. The local section, and a genuine
// differentiator against a national platform running one generic mould page
// for eleven cities (BFD 1.5, competitive context).
//
// Every claim here is about CLIMATE AND BUILDINGS, which is observable and
// uncontroversial. Nothing about health, nothing about risk. The subtropical
// humidity, the closed-up holiday home and the poorly ventilated bathroom
// are three things any local reader will recognise immediately, which is the
// point.
//
// Deliberately not mentioned: the 2022 floods. Flood-affected mould is a
// remediation job that section 5 explicitly refers out, so raising floods
// here would invite exactly the enquiry this page says it will not take, in
// a region where that event is still raw. Left out on purpose, not by
// oversight.

// Section 7. What not to do before we get there. Callout `warning`, the
// charcoal panel, which SECTIONS.md reserves for "don't do this" rather than
// "worth knowing". This is the one block on the site that genuinely earns it.
//
// STRICTLY LIMITED, on purpose. Two things, both well documented, neither of
// them a method:
//   1. Do not dry-brush or dry-scrape it. Disturbing it dry sends it into
//      the air and around the room.
//   2. Never mix bleach with ammonia or with other cleaners. This is a
//      standard household chemical safety warning and the reason it is here
//      is that "mould" and "bleach" are the two words most likely to appear
//      together in a search that lands on this page.
//
// DO NOT EXPAND THIS INTO A HOW-TO. BFD 5.3 bars advice that could cause
// harm if followed incorrectly, and a mould-removal method published by a
// cleaning company is precisely that. No products, no dilutions, no PPE
// instructions. If someone wants the job done, the answer is to book it.
//
// ⚠️ NOW THREE THINGS, NOT TWO, from 15 Sep 2026. The third is the bleach
// one, and it is added rather than expanded into a method for a specific
// reason worth stating: assuming bleach has fixed it is the single most
// common thing somebody does before ringing a cleaner, and the whole
// argument of section 8 falls over if the reader believes the stain lifting
// means the problem is gone.
//
// IT STAYS INSIDE THE RULE ABOVE. It names no method, no dilution, no
// product to use and no PPE. It says what does NOT reliably work, it repeats
// the do-not-mix warning for the other bottle people reach for, and it
// attributes the claim to Queensland Health rather than to TLB, which is
// exactly what BFD §14 asks for and what BFD §5.3 requires of anything
// touching products and safety. Source: Queensland Health, "Cleaning up
// mould after a disaster",
// https://www.health.qld.gov.au/public-health/disaster/public-health-advice/mould
//
// The heading changes from "Two" to "Three" with it. If a fourth is ever
// proposed, re-read the paragraph above this one first.
//
// ⚠️ One word in the first paragraph, same date: "it leaves the surface"
// became "the spores leave the surface". `spores` is a missing entity every
// competing page on this SERP carries, and the sentence is more accurate
// with it.

// Section 8. Why it comes back. The anchor target for pathway card 3, and
// the honest centre of the page.
//
// This section is the reason the page cannot make the promise a competitor
// makes. It should never be softened into "and then it is gone for good".
// See the file header, constraint 2.
//
// ⚠️ Paragraph two picked up the dehumidifier clause on 15 Sep 2026.
// `dehumidifier` is a missing entity that appears across every ranking page
// in this market and appeared nowhere here, and it belongs in this list
// rather than anywhere else because it is one of the cheap fixes. Note it is
// worded as a thing to run, not as a product recommendation.
//
// ⚠️ Paragraph four is NEW, same date, and it is the only paragraph on this
// page that sells the outcome rather than the service. BFD §1.5. The page
// argues its honest position extremely well and never once said what living
// with this actually looks like once TLB is involved, which left the whole
// section reading as a list of things that will not work. It stays carefully
// inside constraint 2 in the file header: it promises noticing and routine,
// not that the mould stops coming back.

// Section 9. The comparison table. Draws the scope boundary in public, in
// the format the site already uses to answer "which service do I need".
//
// The third column is NOT a TLB service, and that is the point of including
// it: a visitor whose problem is in the third column needs to recognise
// themselves there and go elsewhere. A table that only listed the two
// services TLB sells would quietly imply TLB covers everything.
//
// [CONFIRM the first column with Teagan], particularly the "How long" and
// "What it does not do" rows.

// Section 9b. Mould cleaning when it is not your own house. NEW, 15 Sep 2026.
//
// WHY IT EXISTS. `commercial mould cleaning` (150/mo, mapping workbook,
// 10 Aug 2026) is the only mapped secondary with a measured figure that this
// page did not serve in any way at all — every word above it is written for
// somebody standing in their own bathroom. This section is what takes the
// page from 1,220 of 1,370 mapped monthly searches covered to the full
// 1,370.
//
// WHY IT IS A LIST AND NOT A TEXTBLOCK. The copy was drafted as five
// paragraphs of prose with four inline links in it, to
// /house-cleaning/real-estate-cleaning/, /airbnb-holiday-home-cleaning/, /commercial-cleaning/ and
// /commercial-cleaning/strata-cleaning/. NO component in this library renders a
// link inside a paragraph: TextBlock, Callout and ContentGrid all render
// each body string as <p>{paragraph}</p>, so Astro escapes any markup in it.
// deep-cleaning.astro hit the same wall and solved it by running the link
// through a component's existing link affordance instead, which is what this
// does. Three of the four links are live here as row targets, and the copy
// is the drafted copy with each audience lifted out of its opening clause
// and into the row title, which is the only edit the shape required.
//
// ⚠️ TWO THINGS THE SHAPE COST, both worth knowing before anyone "tidies"
// this:
//   1. /commercial-cleaning/strata-cleaning/ has no row of its own, so strata is
//      named in row 3's copy but the row links to /commercial-cleaning/.
//      Splitting row 3 in two would mean writing copy nobody briefed.
//   2. The drafted closing line ("One provider, one point of contact...")
//      sits at the end of the LEAD rather than under the rows, because the
//      `list` variant has no footnote slot. It reads as a promise either
//      way; it read slightly better last.
// Both close for free the day a body string can carry a link.
//
// ⚠️ H2 RENAMED 16 Sep 2026, one word, same instruction as the H1: it read
// "Mould cleaning for agencies…" and now reads "Mould cleaning services
// for agencies…", so the page's only commercial heading carries the full
// primary rather than the `mould cleaning` fragment of it. The heading text
// itself lives on the <ServiceBlocks> call, not up here.
//
// NOTHING HERE IS A NEW CLAIM. Every row describes the same surface work the
// rest of the page describes, under different scheduling pressure. The
// out-of-hours and booking-calendar lines match what the four commercial
// pages already say, and no row implies remediation, an inspection or a
// report. Constraints 1 to 3 in the file header apply to this section
// unchanged.

// Section 10. The health question. SHORT, and it defers rather than answers.
//
// This is the block that keeps the page inside BFD 5.3. Someone searching
// mould at eleven at night is often worried about their family, and the
// honest, compliant and genuinely kind response is to say plainly that a
// cleaning company is not who should be answering that, and to name who is.
//
// DO NOT let this section grow into a symptoms list, a risk explainer or a
// reassurance. Any sentence that begins to characterise what mould does to
// people does not belong on a cleaning company's website. If TLB ever wants
// to cover this properly it belongs in a guide, written with a citable
// health authority source per BFD 3.3 and 5.2, not here.
//
// ⚠️ A SECOND VERSION OF PARAGRAPH TWO IS WIRED BELOW AND GATED OFF, from
// 15 Sep 2026. Read this before touching the flag.
//
// THE PROBLEM IT ANSWERS. Every competitor on this SERP leads with health
// risk. Declining the question and then pointing nowhere specific reads as
// evasive rather than as careful, which is the opposite of what the refusal
// is for. The gated version quotes ONE sentence of NSW Health's own position
// and attributes it to them by name, so TLB is reporting a health authority
// rather than making a health claim. That is the only form of this that
// stays inside constraint 1 in the file header, and it is what BFD §3.3 and
// §5.2 ask for: a citable authority, quoted, not paraphrased into advice.
//
// ⚠️ WHY IT DOES NOT RENDER. It moves the page from "we do not answer that"
// to "here is what the health authority says", and that is a position change
// rather than a copy change. It is Teagan's to approve and nobody else's.
// `healthSourceApproved` stays false until she has said yes, and while it is
// false the original two paragraphs stand exactly as they were. Flip the one
// flag when she does.
//
// If the sentence is ever edited, re-check it against the live fact sheet
// first. It is a quotation and it has to stay one.
//
// ⚠️ THE TWO LINKS ARE NOT LIVE. The copy asks for "NSW Health" to link to
// https://www.health.nsw.gov.au/environment/factsheets/Pages/mould.aspx and
// "Queensland Health" to
// https://www.health.qld.gov.au/public-health/disaster/public-health-advice/mould
// TextBlock renders each body string as <p>{paragraph}</p>, so markup in it
// is escaped — the same limit section 9b works around. The copy says the
// fact sheet is worth two minutes and then cannot hand the reader to it,
// which is the weakest part of this change and the reason inline links in a
// body string are now the most useful component job outstanding on the site.
const healthSourceApproved = false;

// Section 11. Credibility signals.
//
// ⚠️ THE IMAGES WIRED HERE ARE AI-GENERATED, NOT PHOTOGRAPHS OF TLB JOBS.
// They are photorealistic, so unlike the old SAMPLE-badged vectors nothing in
// the frame marks them as illustrations: the `note`, the alt text and the
// heading carry that on their own. The guardrail every page in this build
// shares has not moved: a fabricated "before and after from a real job" is a
// false trust claim, not filler. The section's `note` prop says so on the
// page itself, in the reader's own view, not just here in a comment.
//
// This is a DRAGGABLE COMPARISON SLIDER (BeforeAfter.astro), not the
// PhotoGallery grid this section used to be — the same change house-cleaning
// made and for the same reason. A grid lays four independent frames out and
// leaves the viewer to hold one in memory while looking at the next; the
// slider makes the PAIR the unit and does the comparison for them, in place.
//
// Shoot each pair from ONE FIXED POSITION. The slider wipes one frame over
// the other in place, so a shifted camera or a different crop reads as a
// glitch rather than as cleaning — a requirement the component cannot
// enforce and the brief has to.
//
// The photo brief, which the generated frames stand in for:
//   1. BEFORE. Mould spotting along a bathroom ceiling and cornice.
//   2. AFTER.  The same ceiling, same angle, same light.
//   3. BEFORE. Mould in shower grout and along the screen.
//   4. AFTER.  The same shower, same angle.
//
// Two before/after pairs. Consent conditions are the same as every other
// page (written permission, no identifying detail in frame) with one extra
// consideration: a mould photo says something about how a specific home has
// been kept, so the privacy bar under BFD 5.3 is higher, not lower, than for
// a kitchen. Get permission in writing and keep the frame tight.

// Section 12. Booking. TextBlock `dark`, carrying the page's second CTA,
// same placement logic as the sibling pages.
//
// [CONFIRM] the photo-before-quoting flow in paragraph two. It is drafted,
// and it is the sensible way to quote this particular service, but it is a
// claim about how TLB's booking process works and it should match what the
// booking form actually asks for.

// Section 14. Where we clean. The shared 56-town, three-region list from
// src/data/locations.ts. Open questions are flagged there, where one fix
// serves every page.

// Section 15. FAQs. Eleven questions from 15 Sep 2026 (was ten), one block,
// matching the deep-cleaning and end-of-lease pages. BFD 3.5 asks for
// self-contained 40 to 80 word answers.
//
// EVERY ANSWER IS DRAFTED, not briefed. Two carry hard compliance lines and
// are flagged individually. Note that the health question is answered by
// deferral, exactly as section 10 does, and the "will it come back" question
// is answered honestly rather than reassuringly. Both of those are the point
// of the page, not a weakness in it.
//
// ⚠️ THERE IS NO FAQPage SCHEMA ON THIS PAGE, and that is not a gap this
// page can close on its own. Checked against the built output on 15 Sep
// 2026: the only JSON-LD here is Base.astro's LocalBusiness block, and
// `FAQPage` appears in no rendered page anywhere on the site. Faq.astro
// emits none, so every page carrying this component has the same gap. The
// fix belongs in Faq.astro or Base.astro where it reaches all of them at
// once, not in eleven question objects here. data/guides.ts already notes
// the same thing for the guide pages.
//
// In `faqItems`, on "Will the mould come back after you clean it?":
// The claim-critical answer. Never soften this into a promise.
// In `faqItems`, on "Is mould in my home a health risk?":
// The health answer. Defers, names the authorities, characterises
// nothing. See section 10 and the file header, constraint 1.
// In `faqItems`, on "Does bleach kill mould?":
// Added 15 Sep 2026, and deliberately placed third rather than at the
// end. "Does bleach kill mould" is the question people ask BEFORE they
// ring anyone, and answering it here means the reader meets the page's
// central argument — the product is not the problem, the moisture is —
// at the point they are still hoping a bottle will do it.
//
// Source-backed and attributed, same Queensland Health advice section 7
// now carries, and worded the same way for the same compliance reason:
// it says what does NOT reliably work and names who says so. No method.
// In `faqItems`, on "What if the job turns out to be too big for a clean?":
// [CONFIRM] the referral position. It is the page's central boundary.
// In `faqItems`, on "Can you clean black mould out of shower silicone?":
// [CONFIRM] the silicone position, which is an operational call about
// what the team will and will not attempt. Restates section 4.
// In `faqItems`, on "Can you do the mould as part of a bond clean?":
// [CONFIRM] whether mould work is genuinely bundled into a bond clean at
// no separate charge, or quoted as an addition. The answer below implies
// it is quoted with the bond clean rather than included in it, which is
// the safer reading, but Teagan should confirm which it is.
// In `faqItems`, after the answer to "How much does mould cleaning cost?":
// ⚠️ THE LAST UNANSWERED QUESTION ON THE PAGE, and it stays that way
// until Teagan supplies a price band for a typical bathroom mould clean.
// The answer above is honest and it is not an answer. One sentence with a
// real number finishes it, and nobody but her can supply that number —
// the same call deep-cleaning.astro's gated pricing section makes, and
// for the same reason: an estimated price is read as a price.

// Section 16. Closing block. Drafted. Repeats the page's position rather
// than a promise, which is the only close consistent with everything above.
// Heading and lead unchanged 15 Sep 2026 — the button label is the only edit
// here, and it is the fourth and last instance of it. See `pageQuoteCta`.

// The approved version of section 10's second paragraph, gated on
// `healthSourceApproved` above. Since the move to Pages CMS the live
// paragraph is in the content file, so flipping the flag swaps this in as the
// health block's second paragraph. Once it is approved, move this text into
// the content file and delete the swap.
const healthApprovedParagraph =
  'If that is what is worrying you, ask someone qualified. NSW Health and Queensland Health both publish guidance on damp and mould in the home, and your GP is the right person for anything about your own family. NSW Health’s position, in short, is that most people will not have health problems from contact with mould, and that people with asthma, allergies, weakened immune systems or chronic lung conditions may be more sensitive to it. That is their sentence rather than ours, and the full fact sheet is worth two minutes. What we can do is remove what is on the surface and be straight with you about what we saw.';

// CONTENT lives in src/content/pages/house-cleaning/mould-removal.yaml and is
// edited in Pages CMS ("Mould removal", under House cleaning). This file owns
// the layout. The notes above explain why each section says what it says;
// they stay here because Pages CMS rewrites the YAML on save and would drop
// them. Section headings that the notes say live "on the call" in the
// template are now in the content file too.
const commercialRow = z.object({
  icon: section.optional(section.serviceIcon),
  title: section.text,
  description: section.text,
  href: z.string().regex(/^(\/|https:\/\/)/, 'Links start with / or https://'),
});

// This table is all text, and one of its cells is literally "No" (the deep
// clean does not fix the cause). The shared comparison schema reads "no" as
// a cross, so on this page every cell is shown exactly as typed.
const textComparisonTable = section.comparisonTable.extend({
  rows: z
    .array(
      z.object({
        label: section.text,
        description: section.optional(section.text),
        values: z.array(section.text).min(1),
      }),
    )
    .min(1),
});

const page = await getPage(
  'house-cleaning/mould-removal',
  z.object({
    seo: section.seo,
    hero: section.hero,
    // Its background photo sits behind the trust bar and this block together
    // (the Backdrop below), so it cannot be left empty.
    definition: section.textBlock.extend({ backgroundImage: section.backgroundPhoto }),
    pathways: section.pathwayCards,
    surfaces: section.textGrid,
    scope: section.callout,
    climate: section.textGrid,
    safety: section.callout,
    moisture: section.textBlock,
    comparison: textComparisonTable,
    // ServiceBlocks `list` with an icon on each row.
    commercial: section.linkList.extend({ items: z.array(commercialRow).min(1) }),
    health: section.textBlock,
    beforeAfter: section.beforeAfter,
    booking: section.textBlock,
    reviews: section.sectionHeading,
    whereWeClean: section.tagCloud,
    // Heading only: the paragraphs under it all come from Brand lines.
    brandLine: section.sectionHeading,
    faq: section.faq,
    closingCta: section.callToAction,
  }),
);

const pageMeta = {
  ...page.seo,
  canonical: 'https://tlbcleaning.com.au/house-cleaning/mould-removal/',
};

const business = localBusiness({
  description: pageMeta.description,
  areaServed: 'Northern Rivers NSW, the Tweed and Southern Gold Coast QLD',
  sameAs: googleBusinessProfileUrl ? [googleBusinessProfileUrl] : [],
});

const heroImage = page.hero.image && sized(page.hero.image, '1/1');

const pathwayCards = page.pathways.cards.map((card, i) => ({
  number: i + 1,
  ...card,
  image: sized(card.image, '4/3'),
}));

// Six surfaces make three clean rows. If an editor leaves an odd number, the
// last cell spans both columns rather than sitting alone.
const surfaceBlocks = page.surfaces.items.map((item, i, all) => ({
  type: 'text' as const,
  ...item,
  ...(all.length % 2 === 1 && i === all.length - 1 ? { span: 2 } : {}),
}));
const climateBlocks = page.climate.items.map((item) => ({ type: 'text' as const, ...item }));

// The mould clean (the first column) is the highlighted one.
const comparisonColumns = highlightColumn(page.comparison.columns, 0);

const healthBody = healthSourceApproved
  ? page.health.body.map((paragraph, i) => (i === 1 ? healthApprovedParagraph : paragraph))
  : page.health.body;
---

<Base title={pageMeta.title} description={pageMeta.description} canonical={pageMeta.canonical} business={business}>
  <SiteHeader
    logo={{ label: 'TLB Cleaning', src: logoImg }}
    primaryNav={primaryNav}
    serviceNav={headerNav}
    secondaryCta={headerSecondaryCta}
    cta={quoteCta}
  />

  <main id="main">
    {/* Cut-out figure on transparency, so the section colour shows through.
        The subject is framed waist-up and cropped by the bottom edge, which
        contain-bottom then anchors. Two framing mistakes were made here
        before this one worked, both worth not repeating: a full standing
        figure is too thin for the panel, and empty canvas around the subject
        shrinks it under a contain fit. Crop to the subject. */}
    <Hero
      variant="split-single-image"
      imagePosition="right"
      kicker={page.hero.kicker}
      headingLines={page.hero.headingLines}
      subheading={page.hero.subheading}
      lead={page.hero.lead}
      cta={page.hero.cta ?? heroQuoteCta}
      image={heroImage}
    />

    {/* Trust bar + definition block share ONE photo behind a Cream scrim, so
        it is laid out across both. 90%, raised from 75% on 29 Sep 2026.
        Measured over this frame's own darkest region under the copy column,
        75% gave 4.22:1 — an AA FAIL. 90% gives 5.96:1. Backdrop.astro's
        `opacity` doc carries the method. Re-check it if the photo is swapped
        in Pages CMS. */}
    <Backdrop image={page.definition.backgroundImage} tint="muted" opacity={90} focus="top">
      <TrustBar variant="card" cards={trustBarCards} />
      <TextBlock
        theme="muted"
        align="left"
        heading={page.definition.heading}
        body={page.definition.body}
        cta={page.definition.cta}
      />
    </Backdrop>

    <PathwayCards
      heading={page.pathways.heading}
      headingAccent={page.pathways.headingAccent}
      cards={pathwayCards}
    />

    <ContentGrid
      columns={2}
      heading={page.surfaces.heading}
      lead={page.surfaces.lead}
      blocks={surfaceBlocks}
    />

    <Callout tone="note" heading={page.scope.heading} body={page.scope.body} cta={page.scope.cta} />

    <ContentGrid
      columns={3}
      heading={page.climate.heading}
      lead={page.climate.lead}
      blocks={climateBlocks}
    />

    {/* `warning` is the charcoal panel SECTIONS.md reserves for "don't do
        this". Strictly two things, no method. Do not expand into a how-to. */}
    <Callout tone="warning" heading={page.safety.heading} body={page.safety.body} cta={page.safety.cta} />

    {/* Wrapper carries the #moisture anchor PathwayCards card 3 links to.
        TextBlock takes no id prop, and adding one just for an anchor target
        isn't worth a shared-component change. Same pattern the sibling pages
        use for #compare. */}
    <div id="moisture">
      <TextBlock
        theme="light"
        align="left"
        heading={page.moisture.heading}
        body={page.moisture.body}
        cta={page.moisture.cta}
        backgroundImage={page.moisture.backgroundImage}
      />
    </div>

    {/* ⚠️ OUTSTANDING, 15 Sep 2026: the "Deep clean" column header should
        link to /house-cleaning/deep-cleaning/. ComparisonTable's `columns` take a `label`
        and a `highlight` and no `href`, so this needs the component to gain
        one — it is a shared change across every page using the table, not a
        copy change, and it was not worth making unilaterally from here. The
        table content itself is unchanged and stays that way: it is the best
        routing module on the page. */}
    <ComparisonTable
      heading={page.comparison.heading}
      lead={page.comparison.lead}
      cornerLabel={page.comparison.cornerLabel}
      columns={comparisonColumns}
      rows={page.comparison.rows}
      footnote={page.comparison.footnote}
      cta={page.comparison.cta}
    />

    {/* §9b, new 15 Sep 2026. Sits HERE, after the comparison table and
        before the health question, on purpose: the reader has just been
        shown which of the three jobs theirs is, and a property manager or a
        venue owner reading that table is exactly who the next section is
        for. Putting it any later buries the only block on the page that
        serves `commercial mould cleaning` at all. See the block above
        `commercialRows` for what the list shape cost. */}
    <ServiceBlocks
      variant="list"
      heading={page.commercial.heading}
      lead={page.commercial.lead}
      note={page.commercial.note}
      items={page.commercial.items}
    />

    <TextBlock
      theme="muted"
      align="left"
      heading={page.health.heading}
      body={healthBody}
      cta={page.health.cta}
      backgroundImage={page.health.backgroundImage}
    />

    {/* Drag the handle to wipe between the two frames of each pair — see
        the block above `beforeAfterPairs` for why this is a slider and not
        a gallery, and for the fact that these four images are AI-generated
        illustrations. The `note` is what tells the reader that; delete it
        only when real job photography replaces them. The heading no longer
        says "on real jobs", which these images would make false. */}
    <BeforeAfter
      heading={page.beforeAfter.heading}
      lead={page.beforeAfter.lead}
      pairs={page.beforeAfter.pairs}
      note={page.beforeAfter.note}
      beforeLabel={page.beforeAfter.beforeLabel}
      afterLabel={page.beforeAfter.afterLabel}
    />

    <TextBlock
      theme="dark"
      align="left"
      heading={page.booking.heading}
      body={page.booking.body}
      cta={page.booking.cta ?? pageQuoteCta}
      backgroundImage={page.booking.backgroundImage}
    />

    <GoogleReviewSlider heading={page.reviews.heading} reviews={googleReviews} profileUrl={googleBusinessProfileUrl} />

    {/* ✅ RESOLVED 18 Sep 2026. The copy review of this page read the town
        list as all three groups present and complete per BFD §2.1, and
        reported no regression. For three days that was wrong — locations.ts
        ran a curated list that rendered two groups while this page's hero,
        lead and definition paragraph all named the Tweed. The client's call to
        name every mega-menu town and link only the live pages restored the
        third group, so the review's reading is accurate again.

        Kept as a note rather than deleted because the fix was a site-wide data
        change in locations.ts, not a change here: this page never diverged on
        its own, and the next review should not read the history as a new
        finding. */}
    <TagCloud
      heading={page.whereWeClean.heading}
      subheading={page.whereWeClean.subheading}
      tags={page.whereWeClean.tags}
      groups={locationGroups}
      note={page.whereWeClean.note}
    />

    {/* §1.3 The Line as a section heading, with §1.4 as its standfirst.
        The copy, and the reason this page gets `fast` rather than the other
        variant, both live in data/brand-lines.ts. Change it there, not here.

        `muted`, not `dark`: TagCloud above paints --color-brand and Faq below
        paints --color-surface, so Cream between them keeps the page
        alternating instead of stacking two dark bands.

        No CTA prop. §9.5 wants one primary CTA per page, repeated, and the
        closing CallToAction is directly under the FAQs. */}
    <TextBlock
      theme="muted"
      align="left"
      heading={page.brandLine.heading}
      body={[managedForYou.oneOff, ...theLineBody.condition, ...howItWorks.general]}
    />

    <Faq heading={page.faq.heading} lead={page.faq.lead} items={page.faq.items} />

    <CallToAction
      variant="secondary"
      kicker={page.closingCta.kicker}
      heading={page.closingCta.heading}
      lead={page.closingCta.lead}
      cta={page.closingCta.cta ?? pageQuoteCta}
    />
  </main>

  <SiteFooter
    logo={{ label: 'TLB Cleaning', src: footerLogoImg }}
    columns={[
      { title: 'Services', links: footerServiceLinks },
      { title: 'Locations', links: [{ label: 'View all locations', href: '/locations/' }] },
    ]}
    contact={footerContact}
    copyright={footerCopyright}
  />
</Base>
